    fullStory: [
      `The Scottsboro case began on a freight train, but it belonged to a larger world of Depression poverty, racial terror, and Southern criminal law. Young Black and white people were riding the rails looking for work or survival. A fight broke out between Black and white riders. After authorities stopped the train, two white women accused nine Black teenagers of rape.`,
      `The defendants ranged from early teens to young adulthood: Olen Montgomery, Clarence Norris, Haywood Patterson, Ozie Powell, Willie Roberson, Charlie Weems, Eugene Williams, Andy Wright, and Roy Wright. They were not treated as children or as defendants with constitutional rights. They were treated as symbols of a racial panic that the courts were willing to ratify.`,
      `The first trials moved at a brutal pace. Lawyers were unprepared. The atmosphere was openly hostile. Crowds gathered outside. All-white juries convicted the boys, and eight were sentenced to death. The youngest, Roy Wright, avoided a death sentence only because the jury deadlocked. The case showed how ritual can imitate law while justice is absent.`,
      `National and international attention followed. The NAACP, the International Labor Defense, Black newspapers, labor organizers, and ordinary people argued over strategy and publicity. The case became a global indictment of Jim Crow courts, but the teenagers themselves remained trapped inside prisons, appeals, retrials, and political fights they did not control.`,
      `In Powell v. Alabama, the Supreme Court held that in a capital case, defendants must have effective assistance of counsel. That ruling did not free the Scottsboro defendants, but it changed constitutional criminal procedure. The Court recognized that a trial can be formally held and still be fundamentally unfair if counsel is missing in every meaningful sense.`,
      `The second major Supreme Court case, Norris v. Alabama, addressed jury exclusion. Black people had been systematically kept off juries, even though Black residents lived in the county. The Court ruled that this violated equal protection. Again, the decision mattered beyond Scottsboro. It exposed the lie that a jury could represent the community while excluding Black citizens by design.`,
      `Ruby Bates later recanted and testified that no rape had occurred. Victoria Price did not. The recantation mattered, but it did not immediately undo the case. Jim Crow punishment was not interested only in evidence. It was interested in maintaining racial hierarchy. Even after Supreme Court rulings, retrials continued and convictions persisted.`,
      `The case also exposed tensions inside civil-rights organizing. Different groups fought over who should represent the defendants and how public the campaign should be. Those conflicts mattered because the teenagers' lives were being argued over by organizations with different politics, fundraising needs, and legal strategies. Movement history is not always tidy unity. Sometimes people fight the same injustice while disagreeing sharply over methods.`,
      `The defendants' lives were permanently changed. Years in prison, public vilification, legal uncertainty, and trauma followed them. Some were released or paroled. Some escaped. Some struggled after prison. The phrase Scottsboro Boys can make them sound like a single case file, but they were nine people whose youth was consumed by a legal system that used them as disposable bodies.`,
      `The case also shaped civil rights lawyering. It showed the importance of records, appeals, jury evidence, competent counsel, national publicity, and pressure outside the courtroom. Later criminal-procedure and civil-rights strategies were built in a legal landscape that Scottsboro had helped expose.`,
      `Scottsboro matters because it forces readers to see due process as survival. Counsel, jury selection, time to prepare, public atmosphere, and appellate review can sound technical until the defendant is a Black teenager facing execution. The case is not only about Alabama in 1931. It is about what a legal system reveals when its rituals are allowed to proceed without justice.`
    ],
    costImpact: [
      `The cost was the stolen youth of nine boys and young men, years of imprisonment, death sentences, public terror, family damage, and lifelong stigma. Their families had to fight a legal and political system with far more money, power, and racial authority than they had. Even the defendants who survived did not simply return to ordinary life.`,
      `The impact was national. Powell and Norris became landmarks in the right to counsel and jury discrimination law. The case also trained public attention on how race and poverty shape criminal justice, a problem that did not end with Scottsboro.`
    ],
    whyItMattersToday: [
      `The Scottsboro Boys matter today because wrongful accusation is only one part of the danger. The deeper danger is a system that can move too fast, exclude jurors, underfund defense, tolerate public hysteria, and call the result justice. Scottsboro teaches that fair process is not a luxury added after guilt is determined. It is how guilt must be tested.`,
      `The case also warns against treating legal milestones as happy endings. Supreme Court victories changed doctrine, but the defendants still lost years of their lives. Law can correct rules faster than it can repair people. That gap remains one of the hardest truths in civil-rights history.`,
      `A reader should leave Scottsboro understanding that due process is a human protection. When it fails, the people most likely to pay are those already made vulnerable by race, age, class, and power.`
    ],
