    summary: `Thurgood Marshall is often introduced as the first Black Supreme Court justice, but that milestone came after decades of dangerous, disciplined legal work. Before he joined the Court, Marshall was the NAACP lawyer who helped turn segregated schools, exclusionary universities, unfair trials, and racial barriers into constitutional test cases. He argued Brown v. Board of Education, led the NAACP Legal Defense Fund, and understood that court victories required clients, local communities, records, strategy, and courage. His story matters because he did not simply enter one of the nation's most powerful institutions. He helped change what Black people could demand from the Constitution before he ever wore the robe.`,
