    subject: 'Tulsa Race Massacre',
    category: 'Erasure',
    type: 'Event',
    dates: 'May 31 - June 1, 1921',
    tags: ['racial-terror', 'property', 'oklahoma', 'memory', 'black-business'],
    summary: `The Tulsa Race Massacre was an attack on people, property, and memory. On May 31 and June 1, 1921, white mobs destroyed much of Tulsa's Greenwood District, a Black community known for businesses, churches, newspapers, homes, hotels, doctors, lawyers, theaters, and civic life. The violence began after a young Black man, Dick Rowland, was accused following an encounter with a white elevator operator, Sarah Page. It spread into one of the most devastating episodes of racial terror in U.S. history. This page matters because Greenwood was not important only because it burned. It was important because Black people built it. The massacre, cover-up, and ongoing search for graves all reveal how violence can be followed by official forgetting.`,
    timeline: [
      { date: 'Early 1900s', event: 'Greenwood develops as a thriving Black district in segregated Tulsa.' },
      { date: 'May 30, 1921', event: 'Dick Rowland and Sarah Page encounter each other in a downtown elevator.' },
      { date: 'May 31, 1921', event: 'A newspaper report and courthouse confrontation intensify white mob violence.' },
      { date: 'May 31-Jun 1, 1921', event: 'White mobs attack Greenwood, killing residents and destroying homes and businesses.' },
      { date: 'Jun 1921', event: 'Thousands of Black Tulsans are displaced, detained, or left homeless.' },
      { date: '1920s', event: 'Residents begin rebuilding Greenwood despite obstruction and insurance denials.' },
      { date: '1997', event: 'Oklahoma creates a commission to investigate the massacre.' },
      { date: '2001', event: 'The state commission report documents the attack and recommends reparative action.' },
      { date: '2020s', event: 'Tulsa continues investigations into possible mass graves and public accountability.' }
    ],
    fullStory: [
      `Greenwood existed because segregation forced Black Tulsans to build within constraint. White-owned businesses often refused equal service, so Black residents created their own commercial and civic world. Greenwood Avenue held restaurants, barbershops, theaters, hotels, newspapers, churches, medical offices, law offices, schools, and homes. Prosperity did not make the community safe. In a racist city, it made Black success visible.`,
      `The immediate trigger came after Dick Rowland, a young Black shoeshiner, encountered Sarah Page, a white elevator operator. The details remain uncertain. What is clear is that white rumor and press coverage helped turn the incident into a racial threat narrative. A white crowd gathered at the courthouse. Black Tulsans, aware of lynching danger, also came, some armed, to try to prevent Rowland from being murdered.`,
      `The confrontation escalated. White mobs moved into Greenwood. They looted, burned, shot, and destroyed. Airplanes were reported overhead, and survivor accounts described attacks from above. Homes and businesses were set on fire. Residents fled, fought, hid, or were captured. The violence lasted into June 1 and devastated the district.`,
      `Black resistance is part of the story. Greenwood residents did not simply wait to be destroyed. Some armed themselves because lynching was a known threat and because law enforcement could not be trusted to protect Rowland or the community. Calling the event a massacre does not mean Black people had no agency. It means their defense took place against a larger force of white mob violence and official failure.`,
      `The death toll remains uncertain. The 2001 commission report estimated that deaths could have ranged far higher than the official numbers recorded at the time. Many accounts use up to 300 as an estimate, but precision is difficult because records were incomplete, bodies may have been buried without proper documentation, and officials had strong incentives to minimize the scale.`,
      `After the massacre, thousands of Black residents were detained or displaced. Insurance companies often denied claims, citing riot clauses. City officials considered plans that would have made rebuilding harder. Yet Greenwood residents rebuilt. That rebuilding is essential to the story. The community was not passive after destruction. It fought to return, repair, reopen, and remember.`,
      `Rebuilding did not mean recovery was complete. Wealth had been interrupted. Businesses had to reopen without the capital they had lost. Families mourned under pressure to move on. The rebuilt Greenwood of later decades was real and important, but it existed beside what had been taken and what could not be returned.`,
      `For decades, the massacre was suppressed in schools, public ceremonies, newspapers, and civic memory. Survivors spoke carefully or not at all. The silence was not because the event lacked witnesses. It was because local power made remembrance costly. Official forgetting protected reputations, institutions, and property arrangements.`,
      `The late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries brought renewed investigation. The Oklahoma commission documented testimony and recommended reparations. Archaeological and forensic work has continued around possible grave sites. Public history, museums, lawsuits, and survivor testimony have kept pressure on the city, state, and nation to confront what happened.`,
      `Tulsa is often called a riot in older sources, but massacre is the more honest word. Riot suggests mutual disorder. Massacre names the racial power imbalance, the organized destruction, and the fact that Greenwood residents were defending themselves and their community against white mob violence.`,
      `The Tulsa Race Massacre matters because it joins wealth and terror in the same frame. Greenwood's success did not protect it from racism. It made visible what white supremacy wanted contained. The violence destroyed buildings, but the cover-up tried to destroy evidence. The struggle over Tulsa today is a struggle over whether the country will remember Black prosperity and the violence used to attack it.`
    ],
    costImpact: [
      `The cost included death, injury, homelessness, destroyed businesses, stolen wealth, lost insurance claims, trauma, family displacement, and decades of silence. The attack interrupted inheritance and community continuity. Black Tulsans lost more than buildings. They lost records, tools, savings, networks, and a sense of security that could not be rebuilt with brick alone.`,
      `The impact continues through reparations debates, grave investigations, public history, and the national language used to describe racial terror. Tulsa has become a central example of why property loss and memory suppression belong in the history of racial violence.`
    ],
    whyItMattersToday: [
      `Tulsa matters today because wealth gaps are often discussed as if they appeared naturally. Greenwood shows how Black wealth could be built under segregation, then violently attacked, then denied repair. Destruction was not only a moment of rage. It became policy through detention, insurance denial, obstruction, and silence.`,
      `The massacre also shows why words matter. Calling it a riot helped hide responsibility. Calling it a massacre does not solve the harm, but it names the direction of the violence more honestly. Public language can either protect power or clarify it.`,
      `A reader should leave Tulsa understanding that Black prosperity and Black vulnerability have often existed side by side. Remembering Greenwood before, during, and after the massacre keeps the attackers from owning the whole story.`
    ],
